 "api_error_status": null,
 "result": "Rules reach the app in two phases: the bundle records them when you define them, and replays them when you register it.\n\n**1. Recording (at definition time)**\n- `Bundle.attach_route_rule` in `src/tessel/sansio/bundles.py:413` is the entry point. `Scaffold.route` (`src/tessel/sansio/scaffold.py:344`) calls it.\n- It checks that the endpoint and view names contain no dots. It doesn't touch the app. It calls `Bundle.record` (`bundles.py:224`) with a lambda, which appends to `self.deferred_functions`.\n\n**2. Registration**\n- `Tessel.register_bundle` (`src/tessel/sansio/app.py:571`) calls `bundle.register(self, options)` at line 596.\n- `Bundle.register` (`bundles.py:273`) adds the bundle to `app.bundles`.\n- It then builds a `BundleSetupState` through `Bundle.make_setup_state` (`bundles.py:246`).\n- If the bundle has a static folder, it attaches the static route through `state.attach_route_rule`.\n- It then runs `for deferred in self.deferred_functions: deferred(state)` (`bundles.py:334-335`).\n\n**3. Applying the rule**\n- Each deferred lambda calls `BundleSetupState.attach_route_rule` (`bundles.py:87`).\n- That method applies `url_prefix`, the subdomain, and `url_defaults`. It also prefixes the endpoint as `{name_prefix}.{name}.{endpoint}`.\n- It then calls `self.app.attach_route_rule(...)`, which is `App.attach_route_rule` in `src/tessel/sansio/app.py:606`. That is where the rule is added to the app's URL map.",
